    "content": "Document Reference: PORTPROBE-DOC-GS-02 Document Version: 2.0.0 Classification: Getting Started Guide Status: Official Release Last Updated: November 2026 --- 1. Installation Overview PortProbe can be installed using four primary methods depending on your infrastructure architecture: 1. Building from source with Cargo Universal Linux, macOS, Windows . 2. Manual binary deployment Enterprise Linux servers . 3. Containerized deployment Docker, Podman, Kubernetes . 4. systemd service installation Production Linux servers . --- 2. Building from Source Step 1: Clone the Repository Step 2: Compile the Release Binary Execute Cargo in release mode: The optimized binary with thin LTO Link-Time Optimization and stripped debug symbols will be produced at: - Linux / macOS : target/release/portprobe - Windows : target\\release\\portprobe.exe Step 3: Verify Binary Build Expected output: --- 3. Linux System-Wide Binary Installation To install the PortProbe binary into the standard system path: Optional: Grant Privileged Port Binding Capability If you plan to run PortProbe on privileged ports ports 1024 such as 80 or 443 without executing the entire daemon as root: --- 4. Container Deployment Docker & Podman PortProbe includes a production-ready, multi-stage, hardened Dockerfile based on Alpine Linux 3.20. It executes as an unprivileged service user portprobe , UID 10001 and includes a native health check. Step 1: Build Container Image Step 2: Run Container To run a container listening on port 8080: Step 3: Inspect Container Health Status Output: --- 5. Linux systemd Service Installation For long-running background daemon operation on Linux servers RHEL, Rocky, Ubuntu, Debian : Step 1: Create Dedicated Service User Step 2: Provision Configuration Directory Step 3: Install systemd Unit File Copy the included portprobe.service file to systemd: Step 4: Enable and Start Service --- 6. Windows Installation Step 1: Binary Placement 1. Create a dedicated directory: C:\\Program Files\\PortProbe 2. Copy portprobe.exe to C:\\Program Files\\PortProbe\\portprobe.exe 3. Copy portprobe.toml to C:\\Program Files\\PortProbe\\portprobe.toml Step 2: Add to System PATH In an administrative PowerShell prompt: Step 3: Windows Background Service Setup via NSSM !NOTE While PortProbe exposes a --service flag on the CLI, full Windows Service Control Manager SCM dispatching is currently a roadmap feature see Release Notes . To run PortProbe as a resilient Windows service today, use the Non-Sucking Service Manager NSSM : --- 7. Uninstallation Linux Windows"
