    "content": "Document Reference: PORTPROBE-DOC-GS-03 Document Version: 2.0.0 Classification: Getting Started Guide Status: Official Release Last Updated: November 2026 --- 1. Quick-Start Objective In this 5-minute quick start guide, you will: 1. Start your first PortProbe daemon on localhost. 2. Verify TCP reachability using PortProbe's native --health-check client probe. 3. Verify connectivity using standard network tools Netcat and PowerShell . 4. Inspect connection events in human-readable and structured JSON logs. 5. Stop the daemon gracefully. --- 2. Step 1: Start the PortProbe Daemon Open your terminal and run PortProbe on port 8080 : Expected Terminal Output: !NOTE PortProbe defaults to secure enterprise defaults: bind = 127.0.0.1 , silent reject = true , and hold open = false anti-Slowloris immediate slot reclamation . --- 3. Step 2: Verify Reachability with Client Probe Mode In a second terminal window, run PortProbe with the --health-check flag pointing to 127.0.0.1:8080 : Expected Output: Check the process exit code: --- 4. Step 3: Verify Using External Client Tools Using Netcat Linux / macOS : Output: The connection closes immediately post-banner due to anti-Slowloris default reclamation . Using PowerShell Windows : Output: --- 5. Step 4: Inspect Server Daemon Logs In the first terminal window, observe how the daemon logged the connection lifecycle in real time: --- 6. Step 5: Graceful Shutdown In the daemon terminal, press Ctrl+C . Expected Shutdown Output: Congratulations! You have successfully installed, executed, and verified PortProbe. Proceed to the User Guide ../user-guide/overview.md to explore multi-port binding, CIDR filtering, TLS encryption, and Prometheus metric integration."
