    "content": "Document Reference: PORTPROBE-DOC-EXM-02 Document Version: 2.0.0 Classification: Enterprise Scenario Manual Status: Official Release Last Updated: November 2026 --- 1. Business Context & Objective Business Problem : An SRE team is configuring a high-throughput AWS Network Load Balancer NLB or F5 BIG-IP appliance to distribute traffic across a pool of backend API gateway servers. The team must verify: 1. That the load balancer correctly detects healthy and unhealthy backends via TCP and HTTP /healthz health checks. 2. That load balancer health checks do not leak connection slots or exhaust backend server resources. Objective : Configure PortProbe instances as lightweight mock backend nodes to validate load balancer probing behavior without running complete microservice containers. --- 2. Infrastructure Topology --- 3. Configuration & Execution Step 1: Deploy PortProbe on Backend Nodes 01 & 02 Configure PortProbe with anti-Slowloris default reclamation hold open = false and an administrative HTTP listener for HTTP-based load balancers: Step 2: Configure Load Balancer Health Check - For Layer 4 TCP Load Balancers AWS NLB, HAProxy TCP mode : - Protocol: TCP - Port: 8080 - Interval: 5 seconds - Healthy Threshold: 2 - For Layer 7 HTTP Load Balancers AWS ALB, F5 BIG-IP HTTP monitor : - Protocol: HTTP - Port: 9090 - Path: /healthz - HTTP Method: GET or HEAD - Expected Response Code: 200 Step 3: Validate Load Balancer Target Health Observe the load balancer management console: - Backend 01 10.0.10.11 : HEALTHY Receives 200 OK or completes TCP handshake . - Backend 02 10.0.10.12 : HEALTHY . - Backend 03 10.0.10.13 : UNHEALTHY Connection refused . Traffic sent to the VIP 10.0.1.100:8080 is successfully distributed across Backend 01 and Backend 02, confirming load balancing routing before application deployment."
